body:
The dev-mode warning uses `k in nestedConfig`, but `nestedConfig` can be a non-object if the view config is malformed (e.g., `viewOptions[viewType]` accidentally set to a string/boolean). In that case the `in` operator will throw a TypeError and break rendering in development. Coerce/guard `nestedConfig` to a plain object before using `in` (e.g., ensure `typeof nestedConfig === 'object' && nestedConfig !== null`).
```suggestion
        const flatKeys = [
          'startDateField',
          'endDateField',
          'dateField',
          'groupBy',
          'groupField',
          'locationField',
          'imageField',
          'dependenciesField',
          'progressField',
          'titleField',
          'subtitleField',
          'latitudeField',
          'longitudeField',
        ];
        const safeViewOptions =
          viewOptions && typeof viewOptions === 'object' && viewOptions !== null
            ? (viewOptions as Record<string, unknown>)
            : {};
        const rawNestedConfig = (safeViewOptions as Record<string, unknown>)[viewType];
        const nestedConfig =
          rawNestedConfig && typeof rawNestedConfig === 'object'
            ? (rawNestedConfig as Record<string, unknown>)
            : {};
        const found = flatKeys.filter(
          (k) => k in safeViewOptions && !(k in nestedConfig)
        );
        if (found.length > 0) {
          console.warn(
            `[Spec Compliance] View options use flat properties ${JSON.stringify(
              found
            )}. ` +
              `Move them under options.${viewType} per @objectstack/spec protocol.`
          );
```
